Subject: [PATCH] [mlir] Parse, print and alias nested arrays and dictionaries from a stack

The textual parser, the printer and the alias collection all handle a
builtin array or dictionary nested in another by recursion: each level
of `[[[...]]]` costs several native frames, so an attribute nested a few
thousand deep overflows an 8 MiB stack with a bare segmentation fault.
The bytecode reader has no such limit.

Arrays and dictionaries nested in one another are now handled from an
explicit stack in all three:
- `Parser::parseArrayOrDictionaryAttr` parses an array or dictionary and
  every array and dictionary nested in it, with the same tokens,
  attributes and diagnostics as before; any other element still goes
  through `parseAttribute`. The entry-name half of `parseAttributeDict`
  becomes `parseAttributeEntryName`, which both use.
- `AsmPrinter::Impl::printArrayOrDictionaryAttr` prints them, opening a
  nested one in place unless it has an alias; the text is unchanged.
- `AliasInitializer::visitArrayOrDictionary` visits them for aliases with
  the same order, child indices and depths as the recursive visit, and
  `markAliasNonDeferrable` walks the child aliases from a worklist; the
  dummy printer's array and dictionary cases, which nothing reaches now,
  go.

A dialect attribute's own parameters still recurse through the dialect's
parse and print hooks, as an operation's regions do through its own.
